WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.720
Three numbers run every purification.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.380
Specific activity is enzyme units divided by total protein: the fraction of your

3
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.160
sample that is the target, and it must rise at every good step.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.960
Fold purification is the final specific activity divided by the starting one.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.580
Percent yield is how much of your original activity you kept.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.860
Picture a beaker of mixed marbles: the same number of red marbles in a smaller pile

7
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.660
is a higher specific activity.

8
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:34.113
Four physical properties, four columns.

9
00:00:34.853 --> 00:00:39.413
Solubility: salting out with ammonium sulfate drops proteins out of solution at

10
00:00:39.413 --> 00:00:40.733
different salt concentrations.

11
00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:47.413
Size: gel filtration, where counterintuitively the large proteins exit first.

12
00:00:48.113 --> 00:00:52.413
Charge: ion exchange, where opposite charges stick to the beads.

13
00:00:52.853 --> 00:00:57.773
And binding affinity: a bead decorated with something only your protein recognizes.

14
00:00:57.773 --> 00:01:01.593
Match the method to whatever you already know about your target.

15
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:07.313
Ion exchange separates by charge, and the rule is just opposites attract.

16
00:01:08.053 --> 00:01:13.773
A cation exchanger carries negative beads and binds positive proteins. Use it when the

17
00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:19.273
buffer pH is below your protein's isoelectric point, so the protein is positive.

18
00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:25.353
An anion exchanger carries positive beads and binds negative proteins. Use it when

19
00:01:25.353 --> 00:01:27.733
the pH is above the isoelectric point.

20
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:32.853
Once bound, raise the salt or shift the pH and your protein lets go.

21
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:39.367
SDS coats every protein with uniform negative charge, so the gel sorts by size alone:

22
00:01:40.027 --> 00:01:42.707
small proteins migrate faster, toward the bottom.

23
00:01:43.427 --> 00:01:44.427
Reading it is simple:

24
00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:49.347
fewer bands means a purer sample; a band's position gives its molecular weight,

25
00:01:49.347 --> 00:01:53.787
compared to the ladder; band intensity reflects how much protein is there.

26
00:01:54.107 --> 00:01:59.247
The goal of a purification is a single band: one protein, finally alone.

27
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.410
Mass spectrometry weighs molecules to identify them.

28
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.730
In MALDI-TOF, a laser blasts your protein off a matrix-coated plate and ionizes

29
00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:15.390
it; the ions fly down an evacuated tube, and the time of flight tells you the mass

30
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.650
since heavier ions arrive later.

31
00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.470
It needs only picomoles of sample and can resolve proteins above 10 kilodaltons.

32
00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:28.030
Weigh a protein's tryptic peptides and you can identify it from a database.
